# data-models-relationships

Core Data Models:

1. Inmate Records
- Unique identifiers tied to case numbers
- Bond status tracking with eligibility flags
- Multiple case associations possible per inmate
- Status history tracking for bond changes

2. Case Management
- County-specific case identifiers
- Bond amount tracking with normalization
- Status flags for special handling (PR bonds, no bond)
- Links to agent assignments

3. Agent Records
- Roster system with agent aliases
- Language capabilities (English/Spanish) 
- County jurisdiction assignments
- Availability windows with timezone support

4. Location Tracking
- Geolocation accuracy metrics
- Compliance verification flags
- VPN/proxy detection markers
- Service area boundary definitions

5. Bond Records
- Normalized bond amount storage
- Eligibility status flags
- Review requirement markers
- Special handling cases (PR/No Bond)

Key Relationships:

1. Inmate-Case (1:N)
- One inmate can have multiple active cases
- Each case tracks independent bond status
- Cases maintain county-specific routing rules

2. Case-Agent (N:M)
- Cases can be assigned to multiple agents
- Agents handle multiple cases simultaneously
- Language and jurisdiction rules govern assignments

3. Location-Case (1:N)
- Cases track multiple location verification points
- Each location point includes compliance status
- Geographic boundaries determine service eligibility

Importance Score: 95/100
Justification: These data models represent core business entities in the legal/judicial domain with complex relationships and specialized handling requirements.

